f the witness's testimony  matches what's there in reality michael  is a good man who believes in  justice and the truth however as his  journey continues throughout the season  we'll see him turning into the opposite  a man who delivers injustice and has  disdain for the truth this shift results  in a tragic ending for him the worst  ending for this character the death of  his son who he tried  all season to save in this way the  writers are answering the question they  posited  in episode 1 withholding the truth leads  to more pain  in writing for film and television  there's often a scene where the  protagonist has  one final chance at redemption one final  chance to turn  away from what he or she has become and  right his or her wrongs  in your honor this scene for michael  occurs when he's confronted by his love  interest and former clerk  lee delemere she's found about the  murder cover-up and wants him to turn  himself in this is about  justice justice  down there they don't survive  kofi didn't adam won't if michael tells  the truth he'd be condemning his son to  die  am i a bad person because i value my own  life  so much less than the life of my child  everything michael has done  has been for his son and he's lost sight  of the fact that his decisions have led  to considerable pain and death family  jones and her three children including  kofi  are dead because of him not to mention  trevor the guy who tried to blackmail  michael who ended up getting shot by  jimmy michael has done  terrible things to protect his son and  in this way he kind of becomes like the  series's main antagonist  jimmy baxter michael is capable of going  against all the values he once held dear  to protect his son  and lee tells michael what he has to do  in order to cleanse his soul  do the right thing by convicting carlo  baxter for murdering kofi jones  and maybe his soul will get a second  chance and you're so  it's a second chance michael is really  manipulative  in this scene holding up the phone for  lee to call detective costello on him  but telling her if she does  she'd be condemning his son to die he  even throws a curveball to her telling  her he loves her  but lee isn't having it you can't have  me  you can't ever have me now so michael  has this chance of redemption but he  doesn't take it and this is when i knew  the story had to end in tragedy and i'll  get more into how this ties into the  shawshank redemption and mozart in a bit  so carlo ends up getting off for murder  adam gets a girlfriend and accepted into  nyu  and everything seems to go back for  michael basically all the bad guys face  no repercussions for what they did that  is until the show's final act  celebrating carlo's verdict fia invites  adam to meet her dad and come to their  party at the hotel  she doesn't know that adam was the one  to kill her brother however earlier that  day when jimmy went to console his wife  during the court case  he notices adam using an inhaler the  same object found at the scene of his  son's death  up until this point he thought michael  had been the one to hit and run his son  but now he knows he was just covering  for adam michael gets a call from  jimmy basically implying he's going to  kill his son so he tries to get in his  car but it's blocked by a truck good  thing he's been practicing for a  marathon because he has to run the  entire way downtown  where he's not let in by the hotel's  bouncers so this is just more evidence  of all these  tiny sequences of events lining up  perfectly to reach this  ending you never know when  life is gonna line up just perfectly  and walk you right into your future  michael has to watch through the window  as  jimmy hugs his son unsure of what's  going to happen when suddenly eugene the  half-brother of kofi jones  seeking revenge fires at carlo but  misses and hits adam and the irony here  is insane adam who accidentally killed  someone and ran away is killed by  someone by accident who 